WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.230 --> 00:00:09.790
The completion of the Human Genome Project
lays a foundation for systematically studying

00:00:09.790 --> 00:00:15.320
the human genome from evolutionary history
to precision medicine against diseases.

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:20.070
With the explosive growth of biological data,
there is an increasing number of biological

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:24.560
databases that have been developed in aid
of human-related research.

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:30.890
In this lecture, we will present a brief overview
of the major human related biological databases.

00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:35.210
How the data is being arranged and classified
into different categories according to their

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:36.560
data types?

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:40.150
And, how to retrieve and analyze them?

00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:45.579
As human-related databases continue to grow
not only in count but also in volume, challenges

00:00:45.579 --> 00:00:50.510
are ahead in big data storage, processing,
exchange and curation.

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:52.420
What is a database?

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:57.050
A database is a collection of information
that is organized so that it can be easily

00:00:57.050 --> 00:01:00.239
accessed, managed and updated.

00:01:00.239 --> 00:01:04.760
Databases are composed of computer hardware
and software for data management.

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:09.200
The chief objective of the development of
a database is to organize data in a set of

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:13.410
structured records to enable easy retrieval
of information.

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:18.540
Although data retrieval is the main purpose
of all databases, biological databases often

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:23.000
have a higher level of requirement, known
as knowledge discovery, which refers to the

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:27.140
identification of connections between pieces
of information that were not known when the

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:29.750
information was first entered.

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:34.920
For example, databases containing raw sequence
information can perform extra computational

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:39.950
tasks to identify sequence homology or conserved
motifs.

00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:45.790
These features facilitate the discovery of
new biological insights from raw data.

00:01:45.790 --> 00:01:50.890
According to a report of 2014 Molecular Biology
Database Collection in the journal Nucleic

00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:58.610
Acids Research, there are a sum of 1552 databases
that are publicly accessible online.

00:01:58.610 --> 00:02:03.320
This does not include online services without
publication in peer-reviewed journal or being

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:05.880
developed by commercial companies.

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:11.330
Thus, the actual number of online databases
could well exceed this number.

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:15.849
Considering the continuously proliferating
number of biological databases, it becomes

00:02:15.849 --> 00:02:20.569
increasingly daunting and time-consuming to
navigate in the huge volume of databases of

00:02:20.569 --> 00:02:22.319
interest.

00:02:22.319 --> 00:02:27.620
Considering the heterogeneity in data type,
scope and curation, biological databases can

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:32.629
be classified into multiple categories under
different criteria.

00:02:32.629 --> 00:02:37.730
According to the scope of data coverage, biological
databases can be classified as comprehensive

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:40.980
and specialized databases.

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:44.800
Comprehensive databases cover different types
of data from numerous species and typical

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:51.730
examples are GenBank, European Molecular Biology
Laboratory (EMBL), and DNA Data Bank of Japan

00:02:51.730 --> 00:02:54.010
(DDBJ).

00:02:54.010 --> 00:02:58.709
These three databases were established as
the International Nucleotide Sequence Database

00:02:58.709 --> 00:03:05.030
Collaboration in 1988 to collect and disseminate
DNA and RNA sequences.

00:03:05.030 --> 00:03:10.370
On the other hand, specialized databases contain
specific types of data or data from specific

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:12.269
organisms.

00:03:12.269 --> 00:03:17.480
For example, WormBase is for nematode biology
and genomics, and RiceWiki is for community

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:20.340
curation of rice genes.

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:25.599
According to the level of data curation, biological
databases can roughly fall into primary and

00:03:25.599 --> 00:03:29.219
secondary or derivative databases.

00:03:29.219 --> 00:03:34.719
Primary databases contain raw data as archival
repository such as the NCBI Sequence Read

00:03:34.719 --> 00:03:40.189
Archive (SRA), whereas secondary or derivative
databases contain curated information as added

00:03:40.189 --> 00:03:43.889
value, e.g., NCBI RefSeq.

00:03:43.889 --> 00:03:48.879
As a consequence of the explosive growth of
data, curation increasingly requires collective

00:03:48.879 --> 00:03:52.879
intelligence for collaborative data integration
and annotation.

00:03:52.879 --> 00:03:59.169
Therefore, biological databases can also be
classified as expert-curated databases, e.g.,

00:03:59.169 --> 00:04:04.439
RefSeq and TAIR, and community-curated databases,
which are curated in a collective and collaborative

00:04:04.439 --> 00:04:08.749
manner by a number of researchers, e.g., LncRNAWiki
and GeneWiki.

00:04:08.749 --> 00:04:13.659
Finally, according to the types of data managed
in different databases, biological databases

00:04:13.659 --> 00:04:23.640
can roughly fall into the following categories:
DNA, RNA, protein, expression, pathway, disease,

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:27.970
nomenclature, literature, and standard and
ontology.

00:04:27.970 --> 00:04:33.260
Despite having highly different functions,
almost all biological databases shared similar

00:04:33.260 --> 00:04:34.950
architectual.

00:04:34.950 --> 00:04:38.300
Each consists of three tiers of software.

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:44.780
At the bottom is a database management system
(DBMS) that manages a collection of facts.

00:04:44.780 --> 00:04:49.580
At the top is the web browser that transmits
requests for data to the database and renders

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:52.380
the responses as web pages.

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:57.170
In the middle is a software layer that mediates
between the DBMS and the web browser to turn

00:04:57.170 --> 00:05:02.330
data requests into database queries, and to
transform the query responses into hypertext

00:05:02.330 --> 00:05:05.550
mark-up language (HTML).

00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:10.890
In addition to the Gene Database and PDB,
there are many more biological databases designed

00:05:10.890 --> 00:05:12.800
for different purposes.

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:17.470
The table here shows some of the most commonly
used databases in bioinformatics that might

00:05:17.470 --> 00:05:18.700
help you in your research.

